Software Defined Network (SDN)
    - Approach to networking that uses software-based controllers or APIs to communicate with underlying hardware infrastructure and direct traffic on a network

Also apart of a larger concept called Infrastructure as Code (IAC)

3 Portions of a typical network architecture
    - Control Plane (Decides where data goes)
        Carries the traffic that provides the signals to and from router
        Makes decisions about how traffic is prioritized and secured and where it should be switched to in the network

    - Data Plane (Moves the data)
        Carries user traffic on the network

    - Management Plane (How you configure and monitor the network)
        Administers the routers and switches inside the nework and monitors traffic conditions and the status of the network

If you need to Horizontally scale, working in a SDN would be able to realize you need additional resources and can automate the addition of more in your network.

Since everything is on software, collecting security data will be easier for your security team

Concerns:
    Single point of failure
    High Vulnerability

Three Main Types of SDNs
    - Open SDN
        Relies on open-source Technologies
    - Hybrid SDN
        Employs traditional SDN protocols to operate itself
    - SDN overlay
        Method of using software to create layers of network abstraction that can be used to run multiple virtualized network layers on top of a physical network